/*
  UNTITLED FIGHTING GAME — title screen styling.
  Structure is in menu.cxml; this file is the whole look.

  ===========================================================================
  IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE ENGINE DEMO IN A NEW COLOUR
  ===========================================================================
  The engine's menu is gold on blue-grey slate, lit warm from low-left: a
  studio's demo reel. This is a fighting game, so the screen is a STAGE — near
  black, an ember key on the left and a steel rim on the right, with the centre
  third left dark because that is where the wordmark and the verbs sit. If the
  two menus had come out looking the same, the whole content separation would
  have been invisible to the only person it is actually for.

  WHAT IS COPIED, AND WHY COPYING IT IS RIGHT. The type scale, the two surface
  alphas, the focus-over-hover priority and the "padding is constant across
  every state" rule are all measured findings from the engine's own menu, and
  re-deriving them here would have been re-making the same mistakes. Owning the
  front end means owning the words and the look, not re-learning the toolkit.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FOUR RULES THIS FILE OBEYS, each of which costs real time to rediscover
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. `position: absolute` writes ALL FOUR insets. Style::Edges defaults every
     side to 0 rather than "unset" and UIElement pushes all four to yoga
     unconditionally, so left/top always win over a right/bottom written alone.
     Both absolute rules here write all four as zero.

  2. `gap`, `flex-grow`, `flex-shrink` and the four insets are NUMBERS and
     reject a unit: `gap: 10` is right, `gap: 10px` is a parse error that kills
     the WHOLE sheet and leaves the previous one running. width / height /
     min-width / border-radius / border-width are Lengths and require one. The
     parser reports the RULE's line, not the declaration's, so read every line
     in the block it names.

  3. `font-scale` multiplies the BAKED ATLAS SIZE, 48px (kUIFontAtlasPixels in
     Font.h), and the surface's own scale factor is applied on top of it. So
     0.375 is 18px at the 1280x720 reference this document scales against, and
     anything much above 0.71 is being resampled UP on a 1080p display and gets
     visibly soft. That is the constraint the wordmark is designed around: the
     drama is in the lockup, not the point size.

  4. `border-width` is ONE uniform float and it is PAINT ONLY: there is no
     border-left-width, and a border does not inset the content box, so a child
     will happily paint over it.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE TYPE SCALE — five steps, and nothing between them
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     0.71   34px   the wordmark's main line
     0.58   28px   the verbs
     0.46   22px   EVERYTHING YOU OPERATE — option labels, values, chips
     0.375  18px   the status line, and the `*` default
     0.27   13px   captions, keycaps, the crumb, the wordmark's top line

  The verbs are a step DOWN from the engine demo's, where they are the loudest
  thing on screen because nothing competes with them. Here the title competes,
  and the title should win: a game's own front end that shouted its menu options
  louder than its name would have the hierarchy backwards.

  That fourth step is 0.375 rather than a rounder number because it IS
  kUIFontBodyPixels / kUIFontAtlasPixels — the size the atlas was baked to
  serve, resampled 1:1.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE PALETTE — ten values
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     ember       rgb(226,  78,  52)   the accent: rules, focus, the lit chip
     ember hot   rgb(255, 138,  96)   the lit end of every accent gradient
     ember text  rgb(255, 178, 152)   accent-coloured TYPE, which needs to be
                                      lighter than the accent itself to clear
                                      the contrast floor on a near-black page
     ink         rgb( 14,  15,  19)   what an active control's text sits on
     white       rgb(255, 255, 255)
     text        rgb(206, 210, 220)   a verb at rest
     muted       rgb(124, 132, 150)   captions and the status line
     white 0.10 / 0.16 / 0.04         surfaces, hairlines, and the fading end
                                      of a horizontal highlight

  HOT RELOAD: the running app reads the STAGED copy next to the executable
  (build/bin/<config>/Exported/UntitledFighter/UI/), which every build refreshes
  from this file. Save that copy while the player is running and the change
  appears within a quarter of a second — but this directory is MIRRORED, so the
  next build overwrites it. Copy the change back here to keep it.
*/

* {
  font-scale: 0.375;
}

#titleMenu {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------- the two layers - */

.backdrop {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
  background-image: "Exported/UntitledFighter/UI/art/title_backdrop.png";
  background-size: cover;
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* DIRECTIONAL, not a flat veil. Heavy on the left, under the wordmark and the
   verb column, and fully clear on the right where the stage's steel rim is the
   only thing that keeps this screen from being a black rectangle.

   Two stops of `background-gradient` rather than the stretched PNG the engine
   demo uses for the same job: vColor is already interpolated across the quad,
   so two corner colours IS the gradient and it costs nothing. */
.scrim {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
  background-color: rgba(6, 7, 10, 0.86);
  background-color-to: rgba(6, 7, 10, 0.0);
  background-gradient: horizontal;
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------- the frame -- */

.frame {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  flex-direction: column;
  padding: 44px 64px;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ the header -- */

.header {
  width: 100%;
  flex-direction: row;
  align-items: flex-start;
  justify-content: space-between;
}

.wordmark {
  flex-direction: row;
  align-items: stretch;
  gap: 18;
}

/* THE ACCENT BAR, and it is the loudest object on the screen on purpose.
   `align-items: stretch` on the row above means this has no height of its own:
   it grows to whatever the two lines beside it come to, so changing either
   line's size cannot leave the bar short or overhanging. Fixed heights on
   decorations beside type is how a lockup drifts. */
.mark {
  width: 6px;
  background-color: rgba(255, 138, 96, 1);
  background-color-to: rgba(226, 78, 52, 1);
  background-gradient: vertical;
  border-radius: 3px;
}

.wordmark-text {
  flex-direction: column;
}

.title-top {
  font-scale: 0.27;
  color: rgba(255, 178, 152, 1);
  margin: 2px 0 3px 0;
}

/* 0.71 and not a step higher: see rule 3 above. Everything about this lockup is
   arranged so that the largest glyph on the screen is still inside the atlas it
   was baked from. */
.title-main {
  font-scale: 0.71;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1);
}

.crumb-text {
  font-scale: 0.27;
  color: rgba(124, 132, 150, 1);
  margin: 6px 0 0 0;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------- the stage -- */

/* Takes the slack, so the header stays at the top and the footer at the bottom
   whatever is between them. Without this, opening the options panel moves the
   whole composition. */
.stage {
  width: 100%;
  flex-grow: 1;
  flex-direction: column;
  justify-content: center;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------- the verbs - */

.verbs {
  width: 320px;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 4;
}

/* A verb is type on a BAND. Full width, because a button sizes to its text and
   any highlight drawn on one hugs the glyphs, which reads as a badly-fitted box.
   Padding is constant across every state, so moving focus does not reflow. */
.verb {
  width: 100%;
  /* A verb is one line by definition. Text wraps by default, and a longer word
     in a 320px column would silently become a two-line button whose neighbours
     all shift down. Overruled for the BOUND mode slots below, where the text is
     not typed here and the surprise is expected rather than a bug. */
  white-space: nowrap;
  padding: 10px 16px;
  border-radius: 3px;
  font-scale: 0.58;
  color: rgba(206, 210, 220, 1);
}

.verb:hover {
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1);
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
}

/* FOCUS, not hover, is the state that matters: this menu is driven by a pad,
   where hover does not exist. The band is a two-stop gradient fading out to the
   right — a directional highlight rather than a flat slab. */
.verb:focus {
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1);
  background-color: rgba(226, 78, 52, 0.55);
  background-color-to: rgba(226, 78, 52, 0.04);
  background-gradient: horizontal;
}

.verb:active {
  color: rgba(14, 15, 19, 1);
  background-color: rgba(255, 138, 96, 1);
  background-color-to: rgba(226, 78, 52, 1);
  background-gradient: horizontal;
}

/* A BOUND mode's verb: whatever a future mode calls itself, at whatever length.
   Later in the file than `.verb` on purpose — the cascade is CSS specificity
   with later-wins on ties, and these two rules tie. */
.verb-mode {
  font-scale: 0.46;
  white-space: normal;
}

/* The empty state, when the front end is linked and no mode is. Muted and small:
   it is a diagnostic, not a verb, and it must not read as one. */
.verbs-empty {
  width: 100%;
  font-scale: 0.27;
  color: rgba(124, 132, 150, 1);
  padding: 10px 16px;
}

/* Separates what starts the game from what does not. 16px inside the 320px
   column so it reads as a divider under the play verbs rather than as a rule
   spanning the whole panel. */
.verb-rule {
  width: 56px;
  height: 2px;
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
  margin: 14px 0 14px 16px;
}

/* OPTIONS and QUIT are not the game. A step down and dimmer at rest, so the eye
   lands on the play verbs first and finds these when it goes looking. */
.verb-minor {
  font-scale: 0.46;
  color: rgba(124, 132, 150, 1);
}

/* QUIT: the one verb here you cannot undo.
   DESATURATED rather than reddened, which is the opposite of the engine demo's
   answer and for a reason specific to this palette — ember IS the accent here,
   so colouring the dangerous verb red would have made QUIT the loudest thing on
   the screen and TRAINING the quiet one. A neutral focus band says "this is not
   the accent verb" using the one channel the accent occupies.
   Below `.verb:focus` so the tie in specificity breaks this way. */
.verb-quit {
  margin: 4px 0 0 0;
}

.verb-quit:focus {
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1);
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22);
  background-color-to: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);
  background-gradient: horizontal;
}

.verb-quit:active {
  color: rgba(14, 15, 19, 1);
  background-color: rgba(206, 210, 220, 1);
  background-color-to: rgba(206, 210, 220, 1);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------- the panel -- */

.panel {
  width: 720px;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 4;
}

/* ONE ROW SHAPE. Same height, same label column, every option.
   min-height centres CHILDREN, which is what makes this work here and would NOT
   work on a button: an element's own glyphs are placed at position + padding.top,
   so anything carrying text is sized by its padding. */
.opt {
  width: 100%;
  min-height: 52px;
  flex-direction: row;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 16;
}

.opt-label {
  width: 190px;
  font-scale: 0.46;
  color: rgba(206, 210, 220, 1);
}

.opt-value {
  width: 68px;
  font-scale: 0.46;
  color: rgba(255, 178, 152, 1);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- controls - */

.slider {
  flex-grow: 1;
  height: 14px;
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
  border-radius: 7px;
  border-width: 1px;
  border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
}

.slider:hover { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16); }
.slider:focus { border-color: rgba(226, 78, 52, 1); border-width: 2px; }

/* Vertical two-stop, so the fill has a lit top edge and reads as a solid rather
   than a flat block of colour. */
.slider-fill {
  height: 100%;
  background-color: rgba(255, 138, 96, 1);
  background-color-to: rgba(226, 78, 52, 1);
  background-gradient: vertical;
  border-radius: 7px;
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* THE FILL is the focus cue, not the ring: border-width is paint-only and does
   not inset the content box, so at 100% the fill covers the ring entirely and a
   focused slider would be pixel-identical to an unfocused one. */
.slider:focus .slider-fill {
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1);
  background-color-to: rgba(255, 138, 96, 1);
}

.chips {
  flex-direction: row;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8;
}

.chip {
  padding: 9px 16px;
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
  border-width: 1px;
  border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
  border-radius: 4px;
  font-scale: 0.46;
  color: rgba(206, 210, 220, 1);
}

.chip:hover {
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1);
}

.chip:focus {
  border-color: rgba(226, 78, 52, 1);
  background-color: rgba(226, 78, 52, 0.32);
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1);
}

.chip:active {
  background-color: rgba(255, 138, 96, 1);
  color: rgba(14, 15, 19, 1);
}

/* SELECTED. `.chip.chip-on` rather than `.chip-on`, and it MUST sit below
   `.chip:focus`: a pseudo-class counts as a class, so `.chip:focus` is (0,2,0)
   and a bare `.chip-on` is (0,1,0) — focus would win every one of these and the
   current tier would go blank the instant a pad navigated onto it. Two classes
   ties the specificity and file order breaks the tie. */
.chip.chip-on {
  background-color: rgba(255, 138, 96, 1);
  background-color-to: rgba(226, 78, 52, 1);
  background-gradient: vertical;
  border-color: rgba(255, 178, 152, 1);
  color: rgba(14, 15, 19, 1);
}

/* ...and (0,3,0) for focused-AND-selected, which the tie above cannot express:
   without it a focused ON chip would be identical to an unfocused one, so the
   focus ring would disappear instead of the fill. */
.chip.chip-on:focus,
.chip.chip-on:hover {
  border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1);
  border-width: 2px;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ the footer -- */

.footer {
  width: 100%;
  flex-direction: row;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
}

.status {
  font-scale: 0.375;
  color: rgba(124, 132, 150, 1);
}

/* A status colour that lies is worse than none: this is the line a mode's
   refusal to start lands on. */
.status-bad {
  color: rgba(255, 138, 96, 1);
}

.legend {
  flex-direction: row;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 18;
}

.key {
  flex-direction: row;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8;
}

/* NO FIXED WIDTH. The cap holds a BOUND glyph name that changes with the
   player's hands — "A" on a pad, "ENTER" on a keyboard, "L STICK" vs "ARROWS" —
   and a circle sized for a single letter cuts "ENTER" in half. It sizes to its
   text and keeps a pill radius large enough to round whatever lands in it;
   min-width keeps the one-letter case from collapsing to a sliver, and nowrap
   keeps "L STICK" from becoming two lines inside a 24px pill. */
.keycap {
  min-width: 26px;
  white-space: nowrap;
  padding: 5px 9px;
  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
  border-radius: 12px;
  border-width: 1px;
  border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
  font-scale: 0.27;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 1);
}

.keyname {
  font-scale: 0.27;
  color: rgba(124, 132, 150, 1);
}
